      "updated_at": "2025-12-13T14:27:20Z",
      "body": "Your test calls `addop` improperly.\n\nIf you fix your test to pass `0` and not `256` as the `oparg`:\n\n```diff\n<             (\"TO_BOOL\", 256, 1),\n>             (\"TO_BOOL\", 0, 1),\n```\nit will no longer trigger the C-level assertion error.\n\nThat said, the underlying incorrect call is:\n```py\nseq.addop(39, 256, 1, -1, -1, -1)\n```\n\nIt should be:\n```py\nseq.addop(39, 0, 1, -1, -1, -1)\n```\n\n`TO_BOOL` (opcode `39`) does not take an argument, but the test does the `addop` call with `256` as the `oparg` instead of the expected `0`.\n\nThat's precisely what the assertion says:\n\nhttps://github.com/python/cpython/blob/3e36d375352632be85b9ac3a0eeb075a4e03ef6f/Python/instruction_sequence.c#L121\n\n<details>\n\nAny op has to either:\n- take an argument (`TO_BOOL` doesn't) *or*\n- be a jumping instruction (such as in `for`) or a \"block-push\" instruction (such as in `with`) (`TO_BOOL` doesn't) *or*\n- receive `0` as the argument, which is valid for opcodes that don't take arguments (`TO_BOOL` expected `0`, but `256` was passed).\n\n</details>\n\n@iritkatriel, do you think that `InstructionSequenceType_addop_impl` should throw exceptions for incorrect calls before reaching `_PyInstructionSequence_Addop`? Or is this just invalid, and the caller is fully responsible for abiding the contract?\n",
      "author_association": "MEMBER",
